  "id": "chunk_9",
  "chunk": "address in this paper is whether contemporary LLMs can exhibit the flexible,\ngoal-oriented problem-solving that text adventure games likeZorkdemands, or\nwhether their performance reveals a lack of genuine understanding that Mitchell\n[24] identifies as characteristic of systems that have learned shortcuts rather than\ntrue conceptual understanding.\n2 Related Work\nGames have long served as benchmarks for AI capabilities, with particularly\nwell-known examples including chess [6] and Go [31]. Most prominent successes\ninvolve games with well-defined state spaces and reward signals suitable for\nReinforcement Learning (RL). Although impressive, Mitchell [24] notes, what\nappears to be progress toward AGI may instead reflect Dreyfus’s “first-step\nfallacy”—the mistaken assumption that narrow intelligence exists on a contin-\nuum with general intelligence: “It was like claiming that the first monkey that\nclimbed a tree was making progress towards landing on the moon” [9]. Deep",
